  "live_records": {
    "q-15643": {
      "dom_id": "step-q15643",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=12-6x$ and the point $P(3,-6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(-6,3).$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(3,-6).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =12-6x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-6 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-6\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(3,-6)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-6,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-6)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
