    "q-110347": {
      "dom_id": "step-q110347",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=-3+5{e}^{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$",
      "worked_solution": "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ -3+5{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ 5{e}^{x+1} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-3+5{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =5{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =5{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
